// Typechecks the test suite in `scripts/` — which nothing did until now.
//
// `tsconfig.json` includes only `src`, so `pnpm typecheck` never looked at
// verify.ts: the single file that asserts how the whole model behaves was the
// single file the compiler ignored. It had already drifted into duplicate
// imports and a dead comparison, neither of which failed anything, because
// tsx runs what tsc would have rejected.
//
// A separate config rather than adding "scripts" to the main include: the
// suite is a Node program and the app is browser code, and `tsconfig.json`
// must keep describing the browser accurately — it is what stops a `src` file
// compiling against a global the browser doesn't have.
//
// `vite/client` is listed alongside `node` because `types` REPLACES the base
// config's array rather than extending it. The suite imports application code
// directly (`../src/editor/store` and friends), and `src` uses
// `import.meta.env`, which only `vite/client` types — so dropping it means the
// first check that happens to reach `App.tsx` or `EditorProvider.tsx` fails
// with an error pointing at perfectly good application code, from a config
// nobody touched. It passes today only because the import graph doesn't reach
// them yet, which is not a property worth relying on.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "types": ["node", "vite/client"],
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["scripts"]
}
